1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,863
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,863 --> 00:00:07,960
This is chapter fifteen: a post-mortem on string templates.

3
00:00:08,410 --> 00:00:15,132
String templates were previewed in Java 21, previewed again in 22, and then removed.

4
00:00:15,132 --> 00:00:17,452
They ship in no released JDK.

5
00:00:17,452 --> 00:00:23,854
Yet plenty of articles from 2023 and 2024 describe them as part of the language.

6
00:00:23,854 --> 00:00:30,255
That makes them the perfect case study in how to adopt new Java features safely.

7
00:00:31,155 --> 00:00:35,659
String interpolation is Java's most requested missing feature.

8
00:00:35,659 --> 00:00:40,307
Concatenation is readable at three pieces and unreadable at six.

9
00:00:40,757 --> 00:00:44,186
But the deeper problem was never readability.

10
00:00:44,186 --> 00:00:45,253
It was safety.

11
00:00:45,253 --> 00:00:49,062
Concatenating values into SQL gives you injection.

12
00:00:49,062 --> 00:00:54,091
Concatenating user input into HTML gives you cross-site scripting.

13
00:00:54,541 --> 00:00:59,497
Every language with plain interpolation makes those bugs easier to write.

14
00:00:59,497 --> 00:01:09,410
Making concatenation convenient without making injection convenient is the problem string templates tried to solve, and ultimately what sank them.

15
00:01:10,310 --> 00:01:12,141
Here's what was proposed.

16
00:01:12,141 --> 00:01:15,656
Note, none of this compiles on any released JDK.

17
00:01:15,656 --> 00:01:20,635
You'd write STR dot, then a string with backslash-brace expressions.

18
00:01:21,085 --> 00:01:24,463
The key design choice: a template was not a string.

19
00:01:24,463 --> 00:01:29,960
It was an object holding the literal fragments and the evaluated values separately.

20
00:01:30,410 --> 00:01:34,114
A template processor then decided what to produce.

21
00:01:34,114 --> 00:01:35,744
STR would concatenate.

22
00:01:35,744 --> 00:01:42,857
But a SQL processor could bind the values as parameters, never letting them into the query text.

23
00:01:42,857 --> 00:01:45,450
A JSON processor could escape them.

24
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:49,100
So the safe form would have been the convenient one.

25
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:52,855
No other mainstream language's interpolation works like that.

26
00:01:53,755 --> 00:01:55,295
Why was it withdrawn?

27
00:01:55,295 --> 00:01:56,616
First, ergonomics.

28
00:01:56,616 --> 00:02:03,952
Writing STR dot in front of every template, with a static import, read as noise for the common case.

29
00:02:04,402 --> 00:02:11,783
Second, the backslash-brace syntax was unusual among languages and tangled with existing escaping rules.

30
00:02:12,233 --> 00:02:13,827
Third, scope confusion.

31
00:02:13,827 --> 00:02:17,638
Was it about interpolation, or about safe construction?

32
00:02:17,638 --> 00:02:20,895
The design served both and excelled at neither.

33
00:02:21,345 --> 00:02:23,860
And finally, finality is forever.

34
00:02:23,860 --> 00:02:28,738
Once final, the syntax and the processor API could never change.

35
00:02:28,738 --> 00:02:34,913
The JDK team chose to withdraw rather than ship a design they expected to regret.

36
00:02:35,363 --> 00:02:39,307
A revised design may return, but nothing has been announced.

37
00:02:39,307 --> 00:02:41,016
Don't assume any timeline.

38
00:02:41,916 --> 00:02:43,583
So what do you use today?

39
00:02:43,583 --> 00:02:49,852
For readability, a text block with dot formatted gets you very close to the templated version.

40
00:02:49,852 --> 00:02:56,721
For logging, use parameterised log messages, which also skip building the string when the level is off.

41
00:02:57,171 --> 00:03:04,011
And if you have a spike or training deck that uses STR, it won't compile on any current JDK.

42
00:03:04,011 --> 00:03:07,134
The conversion to formatted is mechanical.

43
00:03:08,034 --> 00:03:12,982
And for safety, which was the real goal, you don't need to wait.

44
00:03:12,982 --> 00:03:17,157
Use prepared statement parameters for every SQL value.

45
00:03:17,157 --> 00:03:20,945
Use a serializer for JSON, never string assembly.

46
00:03:20,945 --> 00:03:24,115
Use an escaping template engine for HTML.

47
00:03:24,115 --> 00:03:26,125
And encode URL parameters.

48
00:03:26,575 --> 00:03:32,354
Every one of these separates structure from data, the same principle templates were built on.

49
00:03:32,354 --> 00:03:35,834
Templates would have made the safe path more convenient.

50
00:03:35,834 --> 00:03:38,009
They were never the only safe path.

51
00:03:38,909 --> 00:03:40,864
Here's the general lesson.

52
00:03:40,864 --> 00:03:43,721
New Java features move through stages.

53
00:03:43,721 --> 00:03:48,381
Incubator modules are experiments, and their APIs will change.

54
00:03:48,831 --> 00:03:52,569
Preview features are complete, but they may change or vanish.

55
00:03:52,569 --> 00:03:53,917
There's no commitment.

56
00:03:53,917 --> 00:03:57,471
That's what preview means, and string templates proved it.

57
00:03:57,921 --> 00:04:01,655
Only final features are permanent and production-ready.

58
00:04:02,105 --> 00:04:04,608
And preview isn't just a warning.

59
00:04:04,608 --> 00:04:15,231
You need enable-preview at compile and run time, and the class files are stamped so they only load on the exact JDK release that built them.

60
00:04:15,231 --> 00:04:19,555
Code compiled with preview on Java 25 will not run on 26.

61
00:04:20,455 --> 00:04:23,078
Preview instability isn't theoretical.

62
00:04:23,078 --> 00:04:26,322
String templates: two previews, then withdrawn.

63
00:04:26,772 --> 00:04:33,006
Structured concurrency: five or more previews, and a substantial redesign.

64
00:04:33,006 --> 00:04:36,713
Java 21 code using it doesn't compile on 25.

65
00:04:37,163 --> 00:04:44,417
Scoped values went through an incubator and several API changes before finalising in 25.

66
00:04:44,867 --> 00:04:50,968
The Vector API has been incubating since Java 16, waiting on Project Valhalla.

67
00:04:51,418 --> 00:04:59,943
And records, sealed types and pattern matching show the process working as intended: previewed, refined, then finalised.

68
00:04:59,943 --> 00:05:04,064
But two of these five cost real rework for early adopters.

69
00:05:04,064 --> 00:05:06,693
That's the base rate to plan against.

70
00:05:07,593 --> 00:05:09,734
So here's a workable policy.

71
00:05:09,734 --> 00:05:17,001
Published libraries: never use preview features, because nobody on another JDK could load them.

72
00:05:17,451 --> 00:05:23,296
Production services: final features only, or preview behind a flag you can remove.

73
00:05:23,746 --> 00:05:28,674
Internal tooling and scripts: preview is fine, because rewriting them is cheap.

74
00:05:29,124 --> 00:05:33,412
And spikes and evaluation: that's exactly what preview is for.

75
00:05:33,862 --> 00:05:36,840
One habit matters more than any of these.

76
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:46,065
Treat any Java article without a JDK version as suspect, and check a feature's JEP status before believing a tutorial about it.

77
00:05:46,965 --> 00:05:48,324
Production rules.

78
00:05:48,324 --> 00:05:52,962
Use text blocks with formatted, and parameterised logging.

79
00:05:52,962 --> 00:06:01,438
Use prepared statement parameters for every SQL value, serializers for JSON and escaping engines for HTML.

80
00:06:01,438 --> 00:06:04,797
Check JEP status before adopting anything.

81
00:06:04,797 --> 00:06:11,114
And run non-LTS releases in CI, to catch removals before your next LTS upgrade.

82
00:06:11,564 --> 00:06:13,716
Never use string templates.

83
00:06:13,716 --> 00:06:16,983
Never ship preview features in a library.

84
00:06:16,983 --> 00:06:22,801
Never treat an API as stable just because it's been previewed many times.

85
00:06:22,801 --> 00:06:25,272
Never trust an undated article.

86
00:06:25,272 --> 00:06:29,018
Never build SQL, HTML or JSON by concatenation.

87
00:06:29,018 --> 00:06:32,684
And never turn on enable-preview just in case.

88
00:06:33,584 --> 00:06:34,213
To recap.

89
00:06:34,213 --> 00:06:39,172
String templates were previewed twice and removed, and exist in no JDK.

90
00:06:39,172 --> 00:06:43,432
Their real goal was safety, keeping structure and data apart.

91
00:06:43,432 --> 00:06:50,906
Today, use text blocks with formatted for readability, and parameters, serializers and escaping for safety.

92
00:06:50,906 --> 00:06:56,773
And remember that preview means no commitment, with class files tied to one release.

93
00:06:57,223 --> 00:07:00,150
Next is chapter sixteen, generics in depth.

94
00:07:00,150 --> 00:07:01,511
Thanks for watching.
